This serves as the base code for the transient Actuator Line simulation. This is generally the code used for the actual study, but a few improvements were made in post (noted in files if changed). 

IMPORTANT: A NUMBER OF THINGS NEED TO BE DONE BEFORE THIS CODE IS USABLE and are listed below:
  1. The AL-Steady case needs to be fully ran beforehand in order to provide a steady state. This is done so that the conditions around the container/spoiler are already set before the turbine is added, so that just the effects of the turbine can be observed.
  2. The 5000/ folder needs to be entirely copied over from the completed AL-Steady case into the AL-Transient case (this will serve as the initial conditions). If the steady state simulation is ran to a different last time step, that needs to be updated in both the AL-Steady and AL-Transient controlDicts                                                                                                                                                                      
  3. The dynamicCode/ folder and the constant/polyMesh folder need to be entirely copied over from the completed AL-Steady case into the AL-Transient/ folder and AL-Transient/constant folder respectively. This is to provide the exact same mesh as the steady case so that the initial conditions can directly map on top of the mesh                                                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                                         
The initial simulation took ~100 hours to run 5 seconds on 1 node. However, an unnecessarily high tolerance was used for pressure and has since been changed (and could be reduced further, as noted in fvSolutions). A test case should be reran to get a better time estimate before a complete test was submitted.
5 seconds was chosen because the simulation on its current settings is rotating at 4 rotations/sec, so 5 seconds gave 20 full rotations of the turbine to allow it time to reach a steady transient state. This was enough time for the simulation, and a reasonably steady transient state was reached ~3 seconds into the simulation
                                                                                                                                                                         
Potential Model Improvements:
 - The nSolvers used and tolerances in fvSolutions could probably be tested a bit more to see how efficiently they can run while still preserving accurate values. This would require a variety of short sensitivity tests, but could be worthwhile to reduce the extreme runtimes of the actuator line cases.                                                                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                                         
